<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">National and Kapodistrian University of Athens</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1837">1837</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as University of Athens</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The National and Kapodistrian University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> is the oldest university in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, founded in 1837 under King Otto. Its neoclassical main building in central Athens was designed by Christian Hansen. It became the main centre for training the officials, lawyers, doctors and teachers of the Greek state, and its students were often active in national politics.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/national-and-kapodistrian-university-of-athens.jpg" alt="National and Kapodistrian University of Athens" width="320" height="171" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Wolf, www.foto-tw.de · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Universit%C3%A4t_von_Athen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The university was founded on 3 May 1837 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-of-greece">King Otto of Greece</a> and was named the Othonian University in his honour. It was the first university in the liberated Greek state and in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and the second academic institution in the Greek lands after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionian-academy">Ionian Academy</a>. It began with four faculties: Theology, Law, Medicine, and Arts, which included applied sciences and mathematics. In its first year it had 33 professors, 52 students and 75 non-matriculated auditors.</p><p>At first it was housed in the home of the architects Stamatios Kleanthis and Eduard Schaubert on the north slope of the Acropolis, a building that now houses the Athens University History Museum. In November 1841 it moved into its Main Building, designed by the Danish architect Christian Hansen in a neoclassical style. The front wing, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/propylaea">Propylaea</a>, was finished in 1842 to 1843 and the rest in 1864. The painter Carl Rahl decorated it. With the National Library of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and the Athens Academy it forms the Athenian Neoclassical Trilogy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After events that forced King Otto to leave the country the university was renamed the National University in 1862. In 1904 the faculty of Arts was divided into Arts and Sciences, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of chemistry followed in 1919. Between 1895 and 1911 about 1,000 new students matriculated each year, and the number reached 2,000 at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, so that entrance examinations were introduced for all faculties from the academic year 1927–28.</p><p>From 1911 to 1932 the university was split into two bodies: the Kapodistrian University for the humanities, named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ioannis-kapodistrias">Ioannis Kapodistrias</a>, the first head of state of independent modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, and the National University for the sciences. In 1932 they were merged as the National and Kapodistrian University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. In the 1960s work began on a campus in the suburb of Ilissia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The university's central administration still works from the Main Building. Many prominent figures studied there, among them the prime ministers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charilaos-trikoupis">Charilaos Trikoupis</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleftherios-venizelos">Eleftherios Venizelos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andreas-papandreou">Andreas Papandreou</a>, and Constantine II, the last monarch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. Two Nobel laureates in Literature studied or taught at the university.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “National and Kapodistrian University of Athens” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A modern Greek culture grew up in the kingdom, with the <b>University of Athens</b> founded in 1837, the long ‘language question’ between the learned katharevousa and spoken demotic Greek, and writers such as Kostis Palamas, Nikos Kazantzakis and the Nobel laureate George Seferis.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-greece">Kingdom of Greece · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
